"""Phase 5b copy, batch 1: check-in, churn risk, weight trend, lift progression readers. Original prose (ADR 0036)."""
from phase5_common import base, emit, s

D = {}
EYE = "Free tool · Client tracking"

D["client-check-in-reader"] = base(
    "client-check-in-reader",
    eyebrow=EYE,
    title="Client check-in reader for coaches",
    h1="Client check-in reader",
    description="Turn a client's weekly check-in into one clear coaching decision, the single lever to change, what to leave alone and a ready-to-adapt reply.",
    intro="Weekly check-ins rarely point in one direction. The scale might be flat while adherence looks great and sleep looks poor. Add this week's numbers and the client's note, and you'll get a single recommended action, the one thing to change, what to keep the same and a reply you can edit and send. The note is also checked for anything a doctor or other professional should see. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="How should I respond to a client's weekly check-in?",
    a="Decide on one move, not several. Safety comes first: if the note mentions something medical, refer out and change nothing. Then recovery: poor sleep, low energy or high stress means easing training. Then consistency: under about 75% adherence, fix one habit before touching the numbers. Only when all three are solid does the weight trend decide whether to hold, push or adjust.",
    sections=[
        s("Why one move matters",
          "When a check-in arrives, it's tempting to respond to everything at once: lower calories a little, add a session, suggest earlier nights, swap two exercises. Clients who get four changes in one week rarely follow all of them, and when the next check-in arrives, nobody knows which change made the difference. One clear move is easier to follow, easier to read the following week and easier for the client to feel good about.",
          "The reader is built around that principle. It works through the signals in a fixed order of priority, stops at the first one that needs action, and tells you what to leave alone so the change stays readable."),
        s("The order the reader checks things",
          ("ol", [
              "**Safety:** the client's note is scanned for words linked with medical symptoms, possible disordered eating, low mood, pregnancy or injury. Any of these means refer out and change nothing in the plan.",
              "**Recovery:** sleep under 6 hours, energy of 2 or lower, or stress of 4 or higher means pulling back training while keeping nutrition steady.",
              "**Consistency:** nutrition adherence under 75%, or fewer than three quarters of planned sessions, means working on one habit rather than changing targets.",
              "**Weeks on plan:** if the plan is less than three weeks old and weight isn't on track, it's too early to change anything.",
              "**Weight trend against the goal:** only now does the scale decide between holding, pushing training or recalculating the plan.",
          ]),
          "This order reflects a simple idea: you can't judge whether a plan works until it has been followed, by someone who is recovering, and who is safe to keep training."),
        s("What each verdict means",
          ("table", ["Verdict", "When", "What to do"], [["Refer out", "The note suggests a medical or mental health issue", "Reply with care, encourage the right professional, change nothing"], ["Pull back", "Recovery signals are poor", "Cut training volume by about a third; keep food steady"], ["Fix one habit", "Adherence or attendance is low", "Agree one small habit; keep targets the same"], ["Too early", "Plan under three weeks old", "Hold everything and collect more data"], ["Recalculate", "Good adherence, wrong trend for 3+ weeks", "Adjust intake by about 100 to 200 kcal, or add steps"], ["Push", "Everything on track", "Keep nutrition; progress training slightly"]]),
          "Every verdict comes with a reply in three tones: warm, direct and no-nonsense. Choose the one that matches how you usually talk to that client, then adapt it."),
        s("Reading weight change for each goal",
          "For fat loss, the reader treats a loss of roughly 0.1 to 1 kg a week as on track. Less than that, after three weeks of good adherence, suggests the plan needs a small adjustment; more than that suggests adding food to protect muscle and energy. For muscle gain, a gain of up to about 0.5 kg a week is on track. For maintenance, anything within about 0.4 kg either way is fine.",
          "These ranges are deliberately broad, because one week's weight change is mostly water, food in the gut and salt. If you have a run of weigh-ins, the weight trend reader on this site gives a better read of the underlying trend."),
        s("Why recovery comes before calories",
          "A client sleeping five hours a night, stressed at work and low on energy won't respond well to a bigger deficit or harder training. Fatigue raises hunger, lowers training quality and makes adherence harder. Cutting calories further in that state often leads to a worse week, not a better one.",
          "Easing training for a few days while keeping food steady gives the body room to recover. Once sleep and stress improve, progress usually resumes, and the client trusts that you noticed how they were really doing."),
        s("Why consistency comes before changing the plan",
          "If a client follows their nutrition 60% of the time, the plan hasn't really been tested. Lowering calories won't help, because the problem isn't the target; it's getting to the target. Changing the numbers also sends a confusing message: that the plan was wrong, when really it wasn't followed.",
          "Instead, ask what's getting in the way and agree one specific habit for the week. Planning tomorrow's meals each evening, keeping a protein option at work, or booking sessions into the calendar on Sunday are typical examples. Small, specific changes are far more likely to stick than general advice to be more consistent."),
        s("Scanning the note for red flags",
          "Clients often mention important things in passing: feeling dizzy during a session, skipping meals all day, not having had a period for months, a pain that's getting worse, feeling low for weeks. The reader looks for words linked with these, and if it finds them, it recommends referring out before anything else.",
          "Word matching can't understand context, so it will sometimes flag something harmless or miss something phrased unusually. Always read the note yourself. The scan is a safety net, not a replacement for your judgement."),
        s("Tone and the reply",
          "The same decision can be delivered in very different ways. Some clients respond best to warmth and encouragement; others prefer a straight answer with no padding. The reader drafts the reply in three tones so you can match the client. In every case, edit the message so it sounds like you and mentions something specific from their week.",
          "Specific details matter. 'Great week' is fine; 'Great week, and three sessions while travelling is no small thing' shows you actually read the check-in. That's a big part of why clients stay."),
        s("A worked example",
          "A client on a fat-loss plan for three weeks reports −0.4 kg this week, 85% adherence, three of four sessions, seven hours of sleep, energy 3 and stress 2. There's nothing in the note. Safety, recovery and consistency all pass. Weight is moving at a sensible pace. The verdict is push: keep nutrition the same and look for a small progression in training.",
          "If the same client had reported five hours of sleep and stress of 4, the verdict would be pull back, whatever the scale said. That's the point of the order: the scale is the last thing to decide, not the first."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What should I change after a check-in?", "Usually one thing. Check safety, then recovery, then consistency; only change calories or training when all three are solid and the trend has been off for a few weeks."),
        ("How long should I wait before adjusting a plan?", "At least two to three weeks of good adherence. Early weeks are dominated by water and routine changes."),
        ("What counts as a red flag in a check-in?", "Mentions of chest pain, fainting, disordered eating, missed periods, worsening pain, pregnancy or significant low mood. Refer out and change nothing until the client has seen the right professional."),
        ("Does the reader store my client's data?", "No. Everything runs in your browser and nothing is saved or sent anywhere."),
    ],
    pitch_h="Review every check-in in one place",
    pitch="In Traiv, check-ins arrive with answers, ratings, weight, measurements and photos beside the client's plan, so each read takes minutes and the reply goes from the same screen.",
    nxt=[("Weight trend reader", "/tools/weight-trend-reader"), ("Client churn-risk reader", "/tools/client-churn-risk-reader"), ("Client check-in builder", "/tools/client-check-in-builder")],
)

D["client-churn-risk-reader"] = base(
    "client-churn-risk-reader",
    eyebrow=EYE,
    title="Client churn-risk reader for coaches",
    h1="Client churn-risk reader",
    description="Read a coaching client's churn risk from check-ins, replies, adherence, results, mood and payment, then send an honest outreach message.",
    intro="Cancellations usually follow weeks of quieter signals. Fill in what you've noticed about one client, from late check-ins to a change in tone or a payment problem, and see a risk level, the next step and a message you can personalise. When their own words suggest they want to stop, the reader responds to that honestly rather than with a sales pitch. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="What are the early signs a client will cancel?",
    a="Look for changes rather than single events: late or missed check-ins, slower replies, adherence slipping, results stalling, a flatter or frustrated tone, or a payment problem. One signal is worth noticing; three or more together usually mean the client is close to leaving. If they mention money, a break or stopping, they're telling you directly.",
    sections=[
        s("Why churn is predictable",
          "Most clients who cancel showed signs weeks before. They check in late, then not at all. Their replies get shorter. Their food logs thin out. Their tone shifts from excited to flat. Coaches who notice these changes early, and reach out personally, keep a meaningful share of clients who would otherwise have left.",
          "The difficulty is noticing. With twenty or forty clients, it's easy to miss that one has gone quiet for ten days. A simple, regular check of each client's signals makes the pattern visible before it turns into a cancellation."),
        s("The signals and how they're weighted",
          ("table", ["Signal", "Points"], [["A week-late check-in", "2"], ["Two or more missed check-ins", "3"], ["Slower replies", "1"], ["Gone quiet", "3"], ["Adherence slipping / dropped off", "1 / 2"], ["Results stalled / going backwards", "1 / 2"], ["Last contact flat / frustrated", "1 / 2"], ["Renewal soon", "1"], ["Missed or failed payment", "3"], ["In the first eight weeks", "1"]]),
          "The total sets the level: under 3 points is low risk, 3 to 5 is worth a personal check-in, and 6 or more is high risk. Silence and missed payments carry the most weight, because they are the strongest signs that a client has already started to leave."),
        s("When they tell you they want out",
          "Sometimes the client says it directly: money is tight, they're thinking of a break, they want to finish up. The reader looks for these phrases and, when it finds one, treats the situation differently. The right response isn't a retention script. It's an honest acknowledgement, a genuine option if one would help, such as a pause or a lighter plan, and a graceful exit if that's what they want.",
          "Making it easy to leave sounds counterintuitive, but it protects your reputation and often brings clients back later. People remember the coach who said, 'That's completely fine, here's a plan to keep going on your own.'"),
        s("What to do at each level",
          ("ul", [
              "**Low risk:** keep the rhythm going. Notice their consistency and wins out loud, and ask about their next goal.",
              "**Worth a check-in:** send a personal message this week. Ask an open question about how the plan fits their life, and listen before suggesting changes.",
              "**High risk:** contact them today. Keep it short, invite an honest answer and offer a quick call to reset the plan.",
              "**They want out:** acknowledge it, offer an honest option if it helps, and make leaving easy.",
          ]),
          "In every case, the message should be personal. A generic 'just checking in' is easy to ignore; a message that mentions their goal or a recent session shows you're paying attention."),
        s("The first eight weeks",
          "New clients are the most likely to leave. They're still forming habits, haven't seen big results yet and may still be deciding whether coaching is worth it. The reader adds a point for clients in their first eight weeks, so that a small slip registers sooner.",
          "A strong onboarding, frequent contact in the first month and a visible early win, such as a first strength increase or a measurable change, make the biggest difference to whether a new client stays."),
        s("Payment problems",
          "A failed payment can be a simple card issue or a sign that the client has decided to stop without saying so. Either way, it deserves a prompt, friendly message. Assume the best: 'Your payment didn't go through, probably a card thing. Here's the link to update it.' If there's no reply, follow up with a personal check-in about how they're getting on."),
        s("Why mood matters",
          "Frustration is often the step before leaving. A client who feels stuck, unheard or that the plan doesn't fit will usually show it in tone before they cancel. Respond to frustration directly: acknowledge it, ask what's causing it and offer one concrete change. Many clients stay after a frustrated message if they feel their coach actually responded to it."),
        s("Building a weekly churn check",
          "Once a week, go through your client list and note each client's signals. It takes a few minutes per client and quickly becomes a habit. Anyone at watch or high risk gets a personal message that day. Over a few months, you'll see which signals predict cancellations in your business and which clients respond best to early contact.",
          "Pair this with the client churn rate calculator to track whether your monthly churn falls as early outreach becomes routine."),
        s("A worked example",
          "A client has been with you for 12 weeks. Their last check-in was a week late, replies have slowed and adherence is slipping, but results are on track and their last message was positive. That's 2 + 1 + 1 = 4 points: worth a personal check-in. A short, warm message asking how the plan fits their life at the moment, sent today, is the right move.",
          "If they'd also missed two check-ins and gone quiet, the score would be well above 6: high risk, and worth a call."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("What are the signs a client is about to cancel?", "Late or missed check-ins, slower replies, slipping adherence, stalled results, a flat or frustrated tone and payment problems, especially several together."),
        ("How should I reach out to a quiet client?", "Personally and briefly. Mention something specific, ask an open question and offer a short call. Avoid generic check-in messages."),
        ("Should I try to stop a client who wants to leave?", "Acknowledge it honestly and offer a genuine option if one would help, such as a pause or a lighter plan. If they still want to go, make it easy."),
        ("Does the reader store client information?", "No. It runs in your browser and nothing is saved."),
    ],
    pitch_h="See who's going quiet before they cancel",
    pitch="In Traiv, your client roster shows new and missing check-ins at a glance, and chat sits beside each client's plan, so a quiet client is easy to spot and easy to reach.",
    nxt=[("Client churn rate calculator", "/tools/client-churn-rate-calculator"), ("Client check-in reader", "/tools/client-check-in-reader"), ("Difficult client responder", "/tools/difficult-client-responder")],
)

D["weight-trend-reader"] = base(
    "weight-trend-reader",
    eyebrow=EYE,
    title="Weight trend reader for coaches",
    h1="Weight trend reader",
    description="Paste a client's weigh-ins to see the real weekly trend against normal bounce: on track, noise, a true stall, too fast, or a recomp the scale is hiding.",
    intro="When a client says the scale has stopped moving, a trend line settles it. Paste their recent weigh-ins to compare the real weekly direction with normal fluctuation, and see whether they're on track, still within the noise, genuinely stuck, losing too quickly, holding water or changing shape while the scale stays flat. You'll get one suggested adjustment, or a reason to stay the course, expressed as a likely range. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="How do I know if a client has really plateaued?",
    a=["Fit a trend line through several weigh-ins and compare its slope with the normal bounce. Weekly weigh-ins of 82.1, 81.4, 81.6, 81.5, 81.3 and 81.4 kg show a trend of about −0.1 kg a week over five weeks: slower than a cut needs, so it reads as a genuine stall if adherence is good and the waist hasn't moved."],
    sections=[
        s("Why single weigh-ins mislead",
          "Body weight swings by one to two kilograms from day to day, and by a kilogram or more from week to week, without any change in body fat. Water, salt, carbohydrate, food in the gut, hormones and training all move the scale. A client who sees one higher reading after a good week often concludes the plan isn't working, when the trend is fine.",
          "The only way to see real change is to look at several readings together and ask whether the overall direction is larger than the bounce. That's what the reader does."),
        s("How the reader works",
          ("ol", [
              "It fits a straight line through the weigh-ins, oldest to newest.",
              "The slope of that line is the trend: kilograms or pounds per week.",
              "The scatter of readings around the line is the noise.",
              "From the noise, it estimates a likely range for the weekly trend.",
              "It compares the trend with what the phase needs: a cut, maintenance or a lean gain.",
              "It checks the context you add: waist, strength, cycle timing, recent salt or travel, and adherence.",
          ]),
          "Daily weigh-ins are converted to a weekly rate. More readings give a narrower range and a more confident read."),
        s("The verdicts",
          ("table", ["Verdict", "What it means", "What to do"], [["On track", "The trend matches the phase", "Hold the plan"], ["Mostly noise", "Too few weeks, or bounce larger than the trend", "Hold and collect two more weeks"], ["A genuine stall", "Flat for several weeks on a cut", "Small intake cut or more steps, if adherence is solid"], ["Moving too fast", "Losing more than about 1% a week, or gaining fast", "Add or remove a little food"], ["Recomposition", "Scale flat, waist down or strength up", "Hold; body composition is changing"], ["Probably water", "Flat or up with cycle or salt/travel flags", "Hold; re-read next week"]]),
          "For lean gains, too slow means adding a little food; for maintenance, a slow drift means a small adjustment back to flat."),
        s("Target rates for each phase",
          "For a cut, the reader treats a loss of about 0.25% to 1% of body weight a week as on track. For an 80 kg client, that's 0.2 to 0.8 kg. Faster than about 1.1% a week is flagged as too fast, because it usually costs muscle, energy and training quality. For a lean gain, about 0.1% to 0.6% a week is on track. For maintenance, anything within about 0.25% either way is treated as flat.",
          "Percentages matter because the same loss means different things for different people: 0.5 kg a week is a gentle pace for a 110 kg client and a fast one for a 55 kg client."),
        s("Recomposition: when the scale hides progress",
          "If the scale is flat but the waist is coming down or strength is going up, the client may be losing fat and gaining muscle at the same time. That's especially common for newer lifters and people returning to training. Lowering calories in this situation would be a mistake; the plan is working, just not in a way the scale shows.",
          "Ask clients to measure their waist every week or two and to log their main lifts. Those two numbers turn a frustrating flat scale into evidence of progress."),
        s("Water: the cycle, salt, carbs and travel",
          "Many women retain water in the days before their period, which can hold the scale flat or push it up by a kilogram or more. A salty restaurant meal, a high-carbohydrate day, a long flight or a hard leg session can do the same for anyone. If one of these applies and the trend is flat or slightly up, the reader treats it as probable water and recommends holding.",
          "Comparing weigh-ins from the same point in the cycle each month gives women a much cleaner trend."),
        s("Adherence first",
          "A stall with 60% adherence isn't a plan problem; it's a consistency problem. The reader notes when adherence is under 80% and suggests fixing that before changing the numbers. Cutting calories for a client who isn't reaching their current target only makes the target harder to reach."),
        s("Talking to a client about a plateau",
          "Clients who think they've plateaued are often discouraged. Show them the trend, not just the latest reading. Explain the bounce in plain terms: 'Your weight has gone up and down by about 0.8 kg, but the line through it is still heading down.' If there's a real stall, explain the one change you're making and why, and set a date to review it.",
          "The reader's client message is a starting point. Add something specific, such as a measurement or a lift that has improved, to make it personal."),
        s("Getting cleaner weigh-ins",
          ("ul", [
              "Weigh in the morning, after using the toilet, before eating or drinking.",
              "Use the same scale on a hard, flat floor.",
              "Weigh daily and use the weekly average, or weigh on the same day each week.",
              "Note anything unusual: a late meal, travel, a hard session, cycle timing.",
          ]),
          "Cleaner data narrows the range and makes the read more confident."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How many weigh-ins do I need?", "At least four, and ideally six or more weekly readings or two to three weeks of daily ones. More data gives a narrower, more confident range."),
        ("What is a normal weekly weight loss on a cut?", "About 0.25% to 1% of body weight a week. Faster than about 1% often costs muscle and energy."),
        ("Why is the scale flat when my client is losing inches?", "They may be losing fat and building muscle at once. Waist and strength are better guides than the scale in that case."),
        ("Does the reader save weigh-ins?", "No. Everything runs in your browser and nothing is stored."),
    ],
    pitch_h="Every weigh-in in one trend",
    pitch="In Traiv, clients log their weight and measurements in check-ins, with progress photos, and you see the trend beside their plan, so reading a plateau takes a glance.",
    nxt=[("Client check-in reader", "/tools/client-check-in-reader"), ("Weight loss percentage calculator", "/tools/weight-loss-percentage-calculator"), ("Body recomposition calculator", "/tools/body-recomposition-calculator")],
)

D["lift-progression-reader"] = base(
    "lift-progression-reader",
    eyebrow=EYE,
    title="Lift progression reader for coaches",
    h1="Lift progression reader",
    description="Paste a client's top sets to read the estimated 1RM trend: progressing, a one-day wobble, a stall, grinding or regressing, with the programming move to make.",
    intro="A single great or poor session says little about real progress. Paste the top set from a client's recent sessions, as weight × reps, to compare the trend in estimated one-rep max with ordinary day-to-day variation. You'll see whether the lift is climbing, having an off day, stuck, being forced or slipping, plus a programming suggestion suited to the lifter's experience. It reads strength only and doesn't assess injuries. Everything stays in your browser.",
    q="How do I know if a client's lift has stalled?",
    a="Convert each top set to an estimated one-rep max and look at the trend across several sessions. If the change stays inside the normal session-to-session bounce, about 2 to 3%, the lift has stalled. If load has gone up but reps have fallen so the estimate is flat, they're grinding. Sessions of 100×5, 100×5, 102.5×5, 102.5×4 and 105×3 read as grinding.",
    sections=[
        s("Why estimated 1RM",
          "Clients rarely do the same weight for the same reps every session. One week it's 100 kg for 5, the next 102.5 kg for 4. Comparing those directly is hard. Converting each set to an estimated one-rep max with the Epley formula, weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30), puts every session on the same scale. 100 × 5 is about 116.7 kg; 102.5 × 4 is about 116.2 kg. Almost the same strength, expressed differently.",
          "The estimate isn't a prediction of what the client could actually lift for one rep. It's a consistent way to compare sessions."),
        s("Normal bounce",
          "Strength varies from session to session with sleep, food, stress, warm-up and motivation. For most lifters, a swing of 2 to 3% in estimated 1RM between sessions is normal. The reader uses about 2% for novices, 2.5% for intermediates and 3% for advanced lifters, whose heavier loads tend to vary more.",
          "A change smaller than the bounce isn't a signal. A trend larger than the bounce across several sessions is."),
        s("The verdicts",
          ("table", ["Verdict", "What it looks like", "Typical move"], [["Progressing", "Trend up by more than the bounce", "Keep the progression"], ["Session wobble", "Trend up, but the last session dipped", "Repeat the weight next time"], ["Stall", "Trend flat within the bounce", "Change the driver: reps, sets or variation"], ["Grinding", "Load up, reps down, estimate flat", "Hold or reduce load; smaller jumps"], ["Regressing", "Trend down by more than the bounce", "Deload and review recovery"]]),
          "The move depends on training age. A novice who stalls usually needs a technique or recovery fix; an advanced lifter usually needs a new training block."),
        s("Worked examples",
          ("ul", [
              "**100×5, 102.5×5, 105×5, 107.5×5:** estimated 1RM rises steadily from about 117 to 125 kg. Progressing.",
              "**100×5, 100×5, 100×5, 100×5:** flat. A stall, if it continues over several weeks.",
              "**100×5, 102.5×4, 105×3, 107.5×2:** the load rises, but reps fall so fast that estimated strength drifts between about 115 and 117 kg. Grinding.",
              "**110×5, 105×5, 100×5, 95×5:** falling by more than the bounce. Regressing.",
          ]),
          "The default example in the reader, 100×5, 100×5, 102.5×5, 102.5×4, 105×3, reads as grinding: the client is adding weight faster than they're building strength."),
        s("Why grinding matters",
          "Grinding is easy to miss because the numbers on the bar keep going up. Clients and coaches feel progress. But if reps fall as fast as load rises, strength isn't actually improving, and the sets are getting harder and riskier. Technique often breaks down on grinding sets, and recovery suffers.",
          "The fix is to slow down: hold the load until reps come back, or use smaller increments. Many lifters progress faster overall by taking smaller steps."),
        s("Stalls by training age",
          "Novices can usually progress session to session for months. When they stall, the cause is often technique, sleep, food or simply jumping too far. Fix those before changing the programme. Intermediates progress week to week or block to block; a stall usually means the current stimulus has run its course, and changing the rep range, adding a set or swapping to a close variation helps. Advanced lifters progress over months; a stall often calls for a short deload and a new block."),
        s("Recovery and the read",
          "Poor sleep and high stress can make a progressing lifter look stalled for a week or two. If the client is under-slept or stressed, the reader suggests fixing recovery before changing the programme. One rough week doesn't justify rewriting a plan that was working."),
        s("Strength and hypertrophy goals",
          "For strength, the estimated 1RM is the main measure. For hypertrophy, it's only part of the picture: a client can grow muscle while their top-set estimate stays flat, as long as total hard sets and effort are rising. If the goal is size, check weekly volume and how close sets are taken to failure before calling it a stall."),
        s("Logging sets well",
          "Log the top working set each session with the weight, reps and, ideally, how hard it felt. Use the same exercise and the same standard each time: the same squat depth, the same bench pause. A change in technique or range of motion can make a lift look like it's progressing or regressing when it isn't."),
    ],
    faq=[
        ("How do I calculate estimated 1RM?", "The Epley formula: weight × (1 + reps ÷ 30). 100 kg for 5 reps is about 116.7 kg."),
        ("What is normal session-to-session variation?", "Roughly 2 to 3% in estimated 1RM for most lifters. Smaller changes aren't a real signal."),
        ("What does grinding mean?", "Load is going up but reps are falling, so estimated strength is flat. The lifter is adding weight faster than strength is building."),
        ("Is this an injury assessment?", "No. It reads strength trends only. If a lift causes pain, stop and get it assessed."),
    ],
    pitch_key="workouts",
    pitch_h="Every set logged, every trend visible",
    pitch="In Traiv, clients log their sets in the app against the plan you wrote, so you can see whether a lift is climbing, grinding or stalled without copying numbers into a spreadsheet.",
    keep=[("Workout plans in Traiv", "/features/plan-builder"), ("All free tools", "/tools")],
    nxt=[("One-rep max calculator", "/tools/one-rep-max-calculator"), ("RPE calculator", "/tools/rpe-calculator"), ("Weekly training volume calculator", "/tools/weekly-training-volume-calculator")],
)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    from phase5_b1_extra import EXTRA

    emit(D, EXTRA)
